However, forcing the player to use the workaround is poor design.

>> "No farming at all."
>>
>> My fix causes puddings' maxHP to decrease when they are cloned; in
>> relatively short order, further clones cannot be created and a new
>> "parent" pudding must be generated from scratch in some fashion.
>>
>> The design goal I used was: someone who carelessly attacks puddings with
>> the wrong weaponry should be subject to a certain amount of splitting,
>> because that's why puddings split in the first place -- to add
>> difficulty to killing them.  So, puddings should split up to a certain
>> point, but that point should stop far short of "infinite".
> 
> But your fix will mean that puddings are rendered to harmless. 

You mean they weren't already harmless?  See below.

> In your
> version my underdeveloped character can just split up the dangerous
> pudding so that each split have so little hitpoits that even she can
> kill them with a puny dagger.

First -- vanilla split code cuts current HP in half for each pudding as
well on split, it just doesn't change maxHP.  The tactic you describe
"works" in vanilla, too; if you've ever fiddled around with farming,
that's why people use a ridiculously corroded non-weapon, to try to do
as little damage as possible to maximize splits.

Second, in vanilla puddings are zero-risk monsters; engrave Elbereth,
stand on it, and split like a maniac until the current HPs are very low,
at which point you kill them with a puny dagger.

Third, in my version things are a touch harder, since monsters will
resist Elbereth based on their level and puddings tend to be at least
level 9, minimum.

Will you drop a scare monster scroll?  I'm fine with that; that's
tangible resources, either the scroll itself or holy water to re-bless
it later.

